<!--
    Security floor for the :app module. Android Lint reads this file automatically
    because it is named lint.xml and sits in the module directory; no Gradle setting
    points at it.

    These checks ship as warnings by default, which means a regression would only be
    seen by someone reading the report. Here they are errors, so the same regression
    fails :app:lintDebug.

    The id set is deliberately small: every id below was confirmed to have zero hits
    in this codebase at the time it was added (recorded 2026-10-22), so the gate costs
    nothing today and only fires when new unsafe code appears. Any id added later must
    be verified to have zero hits first, otherwise this file turns existing warnings
    into a broken build for no security gain.
-->
<lint>
    <!-- A hardcoded credential embedded in source. An error because a secret that is
         committed is already leaked and only a build failure reliably stops the next one. -->
    <issue id="SecretInSource" severity="error" />

    <!-- A Cipher obtained through Cipher.getInstance with ECB or with no explicit
         mode and padding. An error because ECB encrypts identical plaintext blocks to
         identical ciphertext, so structure leaks through the ciphertext, and because an
         unstated mode/padding lets the platform default decide the security property. -->
    <issue id="GetInstance" severity="error" />

    <!-- An X509TrustManager that accepts any certificate chain. An error because it
         silently removes TLS server authentication, so every connection is spoofable. -->
    <issue id="TrustAllX509TrustManager" severity="error" />

    <!-- Any hand-written X509TrustManager implementation. Separate from the check above
         because this one fires on the implementation itself rather than on a recognised
         trust-all body: writing a trust manager by hand is where certificate validation
         gets disabled by accident. Verified: this is the id that fires on a hand-rolled
         manager whose checks are empty. -->
    <issue id="CustomX509TrustManager" severity="error" />

    <!-- A HostnameVerifier that returns true for any host. An error because it voids
         hostname checking after the TLS handshake, allowing an off-path attacker. -->
    <issue id="BadHostnameVerifier" severity="error" />

    <!-- A file or directory made world-readable. An error because other apps can read
         it, and the app's whole design assumes its data is private to it. -->
    <issue id="SetWorldReadable" severity="error" />

    <!-- A file or directory made world-writable. An error because other apps can
         modify or plant content the app later reads as its own. -->
    <issue id="SetWorldWritable" severity="error" />
</lint>
